---
scope: src/service/install/installMethodologyDocs
status: aligned
description: "Plans the methodology doc set scaffold — every canonical doc copied byte-faithfully into the host-side hub as an individually idempotent step."
intent: >
  installMethodologyDocs plans the host-side scaffold of the AIDE
  methodology doc set. Each canonical doc lands as its own step in the
  category plan, byte-identical to the maintainer's source, so an agent
  crawling the host hub reads the same bytes every other adopter reads.
  The doc set is enumerated from the canonical registry rather than
  authored here, so adding or retiring a doc upstream propagates without
  edits at this layer. Drift is detected and reported per doc — not as
  an aggregate — so the consumer can pause or skip on a single
  mismatched doc without conflating it with the rest.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Each canonical methodology doc lands as its own plan step, byte-faithful with its source. The hub's content is what an agent reads upstream, with no rewriting, paraphrasing, link rewriting, or composition introduced at install time.
    - The doc set's membership is read from the canonical enumeration the host package already owns. This layer iterates whatever set comes back rather than authoring or pinning the roster locally.
    - Drift between an installed doc and its canonical source surfaces as a per-doc would-overwrite step. The consumer decides per doc whether to confirm or skip; no doc's status is rolled into a sibling's.
    - A single unreadable canonical doc fails its own plan step and leaves every other doc's step intact. One missing source never collapses the category plan into a single failure.
  undesired:
    - Composing or templating any doc's content at install time — including the hub's own entry-point doc. Anything that lands on disk must be a verbatim copy of a canonical source; composition introduces a second authoring surface that drifts on the first upstream edit.
    - Pinning the doc set's membership inside this layer — by hardcoded list, glob shape, count, or filename enumeration. The roster is a snapshot of the current methodology, not an invariant of this submodule.
    - Aggregating multiple docs into one plan step, one status line, or one would-overwrite confirmation. Per-doc granularity is what lets the consumer pause meaningfully on one drift without forcing decisions about untouched siblings.
    - One doc's read failure aborting the category. Independent failures must stay independent, so a partial hub still installs cleanly and the consumer sees exactly which doc could not be read.
---
